@inherits Ti4ComponentBase
@inject IJSRuntime JS

@*
    The start page's backdrop: the senate chamber, one ring of pods per row, turning very slowly.

    Read the four things that were got wrong first, before changing any number here:

    1. The FRAMING is the spec. The lowest ring sits so low that only the tips of its portraits clear the
       bottom edge; the highest one hangs into the top edge so only the lower part of its portraits shows.
       Everything in between is a full ring. That is why the rings are placed by (radius, lift) pairs solved
       for a screen position, not by a pretty formula: `Rings` below carries the solution, `PLACEMENT`
       explains how to re-solve one.

    2. The wall has to get STEEPER as it rises. On a straight cone — every ring the same step up and the same
       step out — all rings sit at the same elevation angle from the camera, i.e. at the same height ON SCREEN,
       stacked into one band. The lift therefore grows much faster than the radius.

    3. The whole ring stays BEHIND the perspective plane (`.senate-scene` pushes the scene back 40vmin). CSS
       magnifies by `perspective / distance`, without bound as a seat approaches the camera plane — and a ring
       drawn AROUND the camera necessarily crosses it. One seat came out 15557px wide that way.

    4. No portrait is ever seen from behind: the near half of every ring faces the chamber's axis, i.e. away
       from us, and `backface-visibility: hidden` simply doesn't paint it. What is left is the far half, and
       because its ends are closer to the camera than its middle, that half arches across the screen — the
       curvature of the rows is the perspective doing its job, not a hand-drawn arc.

    The seats are mounted one frame AFTER the page (see `ready`): laying out ~120 pods before the FIRST paint
    is what left a cold visit sitting on the boot screen until it was reloaded.

    `pointer-events: none` throughout: it is a backdrop, and nothing on it may ever eat a tap meant for the
    buttons in front.
*@
<div class="senate @(ready ? "in" : null)" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="senate-scene">
        @if (ready)
        {
            @for (var r = 0; r < Rings.Length; r++)
            {
                var ring = Rings[r];
                @* The bottom ring is dropped whole (see Ring.Shown). It stays in the array all the same: the
                   offsets accumulate through it, so removing the entry would turn every ring above it. *@
                if (!ring.Shown) { continue; }
                // Each ring leans to face the camera: the ones below it tip their top towards us, the ones
                // above tip it away. Only a fraction of the full elevation, or the outer rings turn edge-on.
                var tilt = -0.6 * Math.Atan2(ring.Lift, ring.Radius) * 180.0 / Math.PI;
                // Every ring stands a little further round than the one below it, and by a bigger step each
                // time (see Ring.Shift). The offsets ACCUMULATE, so what you see between any two rings is
                // that ring's own Shift.
                var pitch = 360.0 / ring.Cols;
                var turned = Rings.Take(r + 1).Sum(x => x.Shift * 360.0 / x.Cols);
                for (var col = 0; col < ring.Cols; col++)
                {
                    var yaw = turned + col * pitch;
                    var slug = Faces[r][col];
                    <div class="senate-seat" style="@Seat(yaw, ring.Lift, ring.Radius, tilt)">
                        @* Most pods are EMPTY — see Faces for why that is arithmetic, not taste.
                           No loading="lazy" on the ones that aren't: the seats are transformed far outside the
                           viewport's own coordinates, so the browser never considers them near enough to fetch
                           and the chamber stayed empty. `decoding`/`fetchpriority` keep them off the critical
                           path instead — this is decoration, the panel in front is what matters. *@
                        @if (slug is not null)
                        {
                            <img class="pod-face" src="@($"portraits/{slug}.jpg")" alt=""
                                 decoding="async" fetchpriority="low" />
                        }
                        <div class="pod"><span class="pod-strip"></span></div>
                    </div>
                }
            }
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// A ring of pods: how far out, how far up (SIGNED, screen sense — positive is down), and how many seats.
    /// <para>
    /// PLACEMENT: the pairs are solved for where the ring lands on screen, against `perspective: 140vmin`,
    /// the scene's `translateZ(-40vmin) rotateX(12deg)` and a 34vmin-tall seat. For a far-side seat
    /// (the middle of the visible arch):
    /// <code>
    ///   y_scene = cos(12°)·lift + sin(12°)·radius
    ///   z_scene = sin(12°)·lift - cos(12°)·radius - 40
    ///   y_screen = 140/(140 - z_scene) · y_scene        // vmin from the centre, positive = down
    /// </code>
    /// The half-height of the window is 50vmin, so the five rings target y_screen ≈ +57, +26, -3, -28, -49:
    /// the first and the last are deliberately past the edge, and the seat's own height (about 19vmin on
    /// screen at the front, 8 at the back) leaves exactly the sliver the design asks for — measured, the
    /// bottom ring shows 21% of its portraits (the tips) and the top one 40% (the lower part). To move a
    /// ring, pick its target y_screen and solve the last line for `lift`; do NOT nudge radius and lift at
    /// once, or the ring changes size as well as position.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Seat counts follow the circumference (a wider ring holds more pods, as in a real chamber) and stay
    /// deliberately modest: every seat is a DOM element on the start page, so ~150 total is the budget.
    /// They are also what decides the PITCH — <c>2π·Radius / Cols</c>, in vmin — i.e. how far apart the pods
    /// stand. Lowering a count spreads that ring out (14 on the 168 ring is a 75vmin pitch where 17 was 62),
    /// which is the price paid here for runs that divide the ring exactly. Raising one crowds it instead.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>Shift</c> is how far this ring stands round from the one below it, as a fraction of its OWN seat
    /// pitch — a fraction rather than degrees because the rings hold different numbers of seats, and what the
    /// eye compares is seat against seat. It grows with height, and 0.5 is the ceiling that means anything:
    /// half a seat is as staggered as two rows can be, and past a full seat they line up again.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <c>Shown</c> false drops the ring entirely — but the entry STAYS in this array, because the shifts
    /// accumulate through it and deleting it would turn every ring above it. <c>Faces</c> is how many
    /// portraits the ring carries, 0 meaning pods with nobody in them; <c>Gaps</c> is how many of its seats
    /// stay empty. See <see cref="BuildFaces"/> — and read the note on the array below before changing
    /// either number.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private sealed record Ring(double Radius, double Lift, int Cols, double Shift, int Faces, int Gaps, bool Shown = true);

    private static readonly Ring[] Rings =
    {
        new(150, 95.2, 16, 0, 0, 0, Shown: false),  // only portrait TIPS ever reached the frame — dropped whole
        // ⚠️ Seats, faces and gaps are the TABLE'S CHOICE (set by the user on 2026-08-13) and they are stated
        // rather than derived. The SEAT counts are what makes this work: each one is an exact multiple of its
        // ring's face count, so every run comes full circle — no gaps needed, and no seam where the sequence
        // wraps. (Getting there cost seats: 17→14, 22→21, 27→24, which spreads the pods a little further
        // apart on every ring. See the pitch note under Cols before changing any of these.)
        new(168, 47.4, 14, 0.18, 7, 0),     // 14 = 2×7
        new(188, -6.6, 21, 0.26, 7, 0),     // 21 = 3×7
        new(208, -64.1, 21, 0.34, 7, 0),
        new(226, -121.4, 24, 0.42, 6, 0),   // 24 = 4×6
        new(240, -174.6, 24, 0.48, 4, 0),   // 24 = 6×4
        new(252, -223.9, 26, 0.33, 0, 26),  // cut by the top edge, so pods without delegates: a face sliced in
                                     // half by the screen edge reads as another copy of itself.
                                     // 0.33 of ITS pitch is what lands half a seat off
                                     // the ring below, because it holds 26 seats against that ring's 24 —
                                     // the two grids don't line up, so measure the stagger, never assume it.
    };

    /// <summary>False until the page has painted once; see the note at the top.</summary>
    private bool ready;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender || ready) return;
        // Wait for the PAGE to be finished — window.load, then one painted frame — not merely for our own
        // first render. The chamber is ~150 elements and 24 photographs; on a cold visit it must not be laid
        // out while the browser is still fetching the app. Falls back to a plain delay if the helper is
        // missing (an old cached index.html), because a backdrop must never be able to break the page.
        try { await JS.InvokeAsync<bool>("ti4.afterLoad"); }
        catch { await Task.Delay(200); }
        ready = true;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Order matters: yaw puts the seat on the ring, translateY lifts it, translateZ pushes it out along the
    /// yawed axis (so it already faces the middle), and rotateX then leans it in place. The lift is emitted
    /// SIGNED — writing `translateY(-{lift}vmin)` around a negative value produced `translateY(--216.7vmin)`,
    /// which is not a valid transform, so the whole declaration was dropped and that ring landed on the
    /// camera and filled the screen.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Seat(double yaw, double lift, double radius, double tilt) =>
        FormattableString.Invariant(
            $"transform: rotateY({yaw:0.##}deg) translateY({lift:0.##}vmin) translateZ(-{radius:0.##}vmin) rotateX({tilt:0.##}deg);");

    /// <summary>
    /// The portrait files used for the seats — file NAMES, not all of them faction slugs (barony/cabal/ghosts).
    /// Hard-coded because the client cannot stat files, and a missing image would leave a hole in the chamber.
    /// <para>
    /// ALL of them, ~1.4 MB together. A subset of twelve was cheaper, but with about fifty seats on screen at
    /// once twelve faces means each one shows up four times and the repetition is the first thing you notice.
    /// They are the same files the agenda wall uses (so a returning visitor has them cached) and they are
    /// fetched at low priority AFTER the first paint, so nothing waits on them.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// ALPHABETICAL on purpose. The rings take their portraits as consecutive slices of this list, so the
    /// order decides which ring gets which faces — and alphabetical scatters the seven that arrived last
    /// (bastion, crimson, deepwrought, firmament, keleres, lizards, obsidian) across four different rings
    /// instead of parking them all in the top one.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly List<string> Slugs = new()
    {
        "arborec", "argent", "barony", "bastion", "cabal", "crimson", "deepwrought", "empyrean",
        "firmament", "ghosts", "hacan", "jolnar", "keleres", "lizards", "lizix", "mahact",
        "mentak", "muaat", "naalu", "nekro", "nomad", "nra", "obsidian", "saar",
        "sardakk", "sol", "titans", "winnu", "xxcha", "yin", "yssaril",
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Which delegate sits in which pod — <c>null</c> is an empty pod.
    /// <para>
    /// A full chamber and one portrait per pod cannot both be had — there are 31 faces against about a
    /// hundred manned seats. The earlier version kept every face unique on screen and paid for it by leaving
    /// two thirds of the pods empty. So the trade is made the other way round now, and deliberately: each
    /// ring gets its OWN handful of portraits (the counts across the rings sum to <c>Slugs.Count</c>, so no
    /// face ever appears in two rings) and simply REPEATS that run all the way round. Repetition within one
    /// row reads as a pattern of seats; the same face twice in different rows reads as a mistake, which is
    /// why the rows stay disjoint.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Gaps are per ring (see <c>Ring.Gaps</c>) and are placed at random within it — a gap is an empty seat,
    /// not a removed portrait: the sequence continues after it (face, face, gap, face, face …), so a gap
    /// costs no portrait. The three lower rings have none at all.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly string?[][] Faces = BuildFaces();

    private static string?[][] BuildFaces()
    {
        var rnd = new Random(1408);   // fixed seed: the chamber looks the same on every visit
        var faces = new string?[Rings.Length][];
        var next = 0;
        for (var r = 0; r < Rings.Length; r++)
        {
            var n = Rings[r].Cols;
            faces[r] = new string?[n];
            var count = Rings[r].Faces;
            if (count == 0) continue;                       // pods with nobody in them
            var mine = Slugs.GetRange(next, count);         // this ring's own portraits
            next += count;

            // ⚠️ A ring is a CIRCLE, so a run of portraits only comes FULL CIRCLE when it divides the manned
            // seats; otherwise the last seat before the wrap is followed by face A out of order — one visible
            // jump per ring, and with 7 faces on 22 seats (22 = 3×7 + 1) that jump puts the same face on two
            // neighbouring seats.
            // This used to be forced by raising the gap count until `(n - gaps) % count == 0`. It is not any
            // more, because it no longer has to be: the seat counts in `Rings` are exact multiples of their
            // face counts, so every run closes with ZERO gaps. Change a seat count or a face count and that
            // guarantee goes with it — the assert below is what says so out loud.
            var gaps = Math.Clamp(Rings[r].Gaps, 0, n);
            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert((n - gaps) % count == 0,
                $"Senate ring {r}: {n - gaps} manned seats do not divide by {count} faces — the run will jump at the seam.");

            var empty = new HashSet<int>();
            while (empty.Count < gaps) empty.Add(rnd.Next(n));

            var cursor = 0;
            for (var c = 0; c < n; c++)
            {
                if (empty.Contains(c)) continue;            // a gap: leave the pod empty, keep the cursor
                faces[r][c] = mine[cursor++ % count];
            }
        }
        return faces;
    }
}
